Moving to the charm one sector of $DN$ pairs, we start with the $D^0 p$ case, which involves 16 coupled  channels, free from the Coulomb interaction. The  $D^0 p$ scattering length from the TROY formalism, $a_0=(-1.58 + \rm{i}0.27)$ fm, is to be compared with the on-shell value of $a_0=(-1.21 + \rm{i} 0.16)$ fm. These sizable values are indicative of the existence of a bound state close to threshold, the $\Sigma_c(2800)$ in $I=1$. Note that the $D^0 p$ interaction also develops a bound state with $I=0$, the $\Lambda_c(2595)$. This state should leave a visible effect in the correlation functions of $\Sigma_c \pi$ pairs, which are not the object of the present study.  The correlation function of $D^0 p$ pairs is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:D0p_TROY}.  The elastic contribution is represented by the long-dashed green line and it is somewhat comparable to the LL result, displayed by the short-dashed red line. We note that the variation in the scattering lengths between LL and TROY explains the different $C(k=0)$ values of the long-dashed green and short-dashed red lines. The full TROY result (solid black line) contains all coupled channels with some thermal weights giving a large contribution at low momentum values (4 channels are below the elastic one, and 11 channels above it, see Sec.~\ref{sec:coupled}).  The cusp observed at $k=88$ MeV signals the opening of the $D^+ n$ channel.
