\subsection{Sub-Stage Division}
The proposed framework divides the entire optimization process into three sub-stages: the first two are the coarse-tuning sub-stages and the last one is the fine-tuning sub-stage. This division allows us to exert different driving forces on guiding particles according to the current sub-stage and implement refined operations on the particles. The mathematical expression of the sub-stage division method is as follows:
\begin{equation}
CSL(k) = \left( k - \frac{k^2}{2^2} \right) \cdot Rate, \quad k = 0, 1, 2
\label{eq:SSD}
\end{equation}
where $CSL(k)$ represents the cumulative step length of the first $k$ sub-stages. $Rate$ represents the proportion of coarse-tuning sub-stages in the entire optimization process. The value of $CSL(0)$ is set to 0 and $CSL(3)$ is set to 1 by default. 

The pseudo-code for the division of sub-stages is given in Algorithm~\ref{algo:3}. To begin with, the cumulative step length of sub-stage array $CSL$ is initialized. Finally, $CSL(k)$ is calculated using Eq. \ref{eq:SSD} (lines 3-5). The difference in the subtraction, i.e., $CSL(k) -CSL(k-1)$, specifically represents the interval length of the $k$-th sub-stage. The interval length represents the proportion of the search time for a particular sub-stage to the total time. As shown in Fig. \ref{fig:2}, it can be observed that the interval length in the first sub-stage is larger than that in the second stage. When the PDD framework exhibits the poor performance of diversity, the value of $Rate$ should be increased to extend the time of the coarse-tuning sub-stage. Conversely, if it is found that the performance of convergence is unsatisfactory, reducing the $Rate$ of PDD is advisable. Generally, the value of $Rate$ parameters should be adjusted according to different problem scenarios.

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\subfloat{\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{fig/PDD/GSDO.png}}
\caption{An illustrative example of the gradual shrinking diffusion operator.}
\label{fig:GSDO}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Gradual Shrinking Diffusion Operator}
Because of the significant concentration differences between the different regions, the particles exhibit a broad diffusion scope across other regions during the initial phase. As the diffusion process advances, these concentration differences gradually diminish and the diffusion range of particles gradually shrinks. Motivated by this phenomenon of particle diffusion, we propose a gradual shrinking diffusion operator to simulate the dynamic process of particle diffusion. The mathematical description of the gradual shrinking diffusion operator is as follows:

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Phi_k &= X_i - \mu^{k-1} \cdot \sigma_0 \\
\Psi_k &= X_i + \mu^{k-1} \cdot \sigma_0 \\
X^*_i &= Unif(\Phi_k, \Psi_k)
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:GSDO}
\end{equation}
where $X_i$ represents an original particle, $X^*_i$ represents the next position of the $i$-th particle, $k$ represents the current $k$-th sub-stage, $\mu$ represents the shrinking rate, and $\sigma_0$ represents the initial deviation amplitude. Assuming that the diffusion motion falls within the interval $[\Phi_k, \Psi_k]$, the left and right endpoints of the interval $[\Phi_k, \Psi_k]$ can be calculated through $\mu$ and $\sigma_0$. The function $Unif$ is used to generate uniformly distributed random numbers in the interval $[\Phi_k, \Psi_k]$. It is worth noting that $\mu$ and $\sigma_0$ should be set to different values to adapt to different problems.

To illustrate the process of gradual shrinking diffusion more clearly, we provide an example in a two-dimensional decision space. As shown in Fig. \ref{fig:GSDO}, the diffusion process for the original particles $x_1 = (5, 12.5)$ and $x_2 = (15, 5)$ is described as follows: 
\begin{enumerate}[wide]
\item As can be seen from Eq. (\ref{eq:GSDO}), assuming $\mu = 0.5$ and $\sigma_0 = 3$, we can obtain $\sigma_1 = 3$, $\sigma_2 = 1.5$, and $\sigma_3 = 0.75$, which correspond to the deviation amplitude for three sub-stages, respectively.
\item In Fig. \ref{fig:GSDO}, $R_1$, $R_2$, and $R_3$ correspond to the diffusion ranges of the $k$-th ($k=1,2,3$) sub-stages, respectively. By adding the above $\sigma_k$ to the original particles $x_1$ and $x_2$, the diffusion range of particles can be determined, i.e., $x^{*}_{1} = \{(5\pm3, 12.5\pm3), (5\pm1.5, 12.5\pm1.5), (5\pm0.75, 12.5\pm0.75)\}$, and $x^{*}_{2} = \{(15\pm3, 5\pm3), (15\pm1.5, 5\pm1.5), (15\pm0.75, 5\pm0.75)\}$. 
\item The next positions of particles are randomly generated through the $Unif$ function in Eq. (\ref{eq:GSDO}).
\end{enumerate}

The gradual shrinking diffusion operator makes use of the parameter $k$ to determine which sub-stage the optimization process has arrived at. Subsequently, new solutions will be generated through varying diffusion ranges.

