As an aside, note that as shown in Lemma~\ref{lem:WhatMinimalExpansive}, expansivity is realized by coding through \emph{any} nonempty proper clopen subset. Thus, for example we can concretely see $\Omega$ as a subshift by mapping $x \in \Omega$ to $y \in \{0,1\}^{F_4 \times C}$ by the formula $y_g = g^{-1}x_0$.

Now induce the action of $(A * B) \times C \curvearrowright \Omega$ to an action of $((A * B) \times C) \rtimes D$ on $\Omega^D$. The subaction of $A*B \times C$ will not quite be minimal, so we will have to restrict our group: Let $G$ be the quotient of the action of $F_2$ by the kernel of the action of $A$ (through the natural isomorphism of $D$ and $A$), and let $\psi : D \to G$ be the projection. By Lemma~\ref{lem:WhatMinimalExpansive}, $G$ contains a nonabelian free group. Let $E \leq D$ be a $\psi$-section of a subgroup of $G$ isomorphic to $F_2$. Restrict the group to $((A * B) \times C) \rtimes E$.

By \eqref{eq:KAction}, the action of $g \in A$ is given by
\[ (g * x)_h = g \cdot (x_h) \]
(i.e.\ it is the diagonal action) for $h \in E$, while the action of $g \in B$ is given by
\[ (g * x)_h = h^{-1}gh \cdot (x_h). \]
% kz_h = \phi(h^{-1})(k) z_h.

Consider now $x \in \Omega^E$ and a finite subset $F \subset E$. Our  we can find an element of $A * B$ whose action moves each $x_g$ very close to any other such tuple $(y_1, \ldots, y_d)$, simultaneously for all $g \in F$.

If $x_g \neq x_h$ for all $g \neq h$, $g, h \in F$, then property P of the action of $A$, and the fact $A$ acts diagonally (by the formula above), directly imply the desired property.

Suppose then that we have $x_g = x_h$ for some $g \neq h$, $g, h \in F$. We show that then we can use the action of $B$ to separate these points, while keeping all other pairs $x_{g'} \neq x_{h'}$
distinct. We first show how to separate $x_g$ from $x_h$, and then explain how to avoid introducing new identities between the $x|F$

For the separation, by applying suitable $k \in A$, we can get the point $k \cdot x_g = k \cdot x_h = y$ into any desired open set. Then we can act by any $t \in B$ to move the pair to $g t g^{-1} \cdot y, h t h^{-1} \cdot y$. We want that $g t g^{-1} \cdot y \neq h t h^{-1} \cdot y$, equivalently $h t^{-1} h^{-1} g t g^{-1} \cdot y \neq y$

Since $y$ can be anywhere in the space (by a suitable choice of $k \in A$), for this we only need that $h t^{-1} h^{-1} g t g^{-1}$, equivalently $g^{-1} h t^{-1} h^{-1} g t = [h^{-1} g, t]$, has nontrivial support for some $t \in B$. This is equivalent to saying that $h^{-1} g$ is not central. But $h^{-1}g \in E \setminus \{1_E\}$ and $E$ is a free group, so this is clear.

Finally, note that by choosing $k$ differently, we can still move $x_g = x_h$ to $y$, but move all other points essentially anywhere in the space. Our choice of $t$ stays the same, so we can certainly make the choices for where these points land in the action of $k$, so that when the action $g' t (g')^{-1}$ is applied to each $x_{g'}$, we do not get any new equalities.
