\section{Direct and free products}

The following theorems show some of the obvious cases where one cannot find minimal sofic shifts.

\begin{theorem}
Let $H$ be a group, and suppose $X \subset A^H$ is a subshift with at least two points. If $H \leq K \leq K \times L \leq G$ for some groups $K, L, H$, then the induction of $X$ to $G$ is not minimal.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
We observe that we can directly take $H = K$, since inducing $X$ to $G$ is the same as first inducing it to $K$ and then to $G$. We may also assume all letters from $A$ are used in points of $X$.

Let $g \in L$ be nontrivial, let $a, b \in A$ be distinct letters, and forbid from $X^{G/K}$ the pattern $1_G \mapsto a, g \mapsto b$ to obtain a subshift $Y$.

This new subshift is nonempty: For any $x \in X$, define $y \in A^{K \times L}$ by setting $y_{(h, g)} = x_h$ for all $(h, g) \in K \times L$. This configuration contains no forbidden patterns of $x$ since on cosets of $K$ we copy the configuration $x$, and the new pattern does not appear because the orbit of the configuration is pointwise stabilized by $L$.

Then observe that forbidden patterns of $Y$ have supports contained in $K \times L$, to see that a $G$-configuration exists (alternatively, pick a set of left coset representatives and copy $y$ on each coset).

On the other hand, $Y \subsetneq X^{G/K}$, since clearly we can extend $\{1, g\}$ to a set of coset representatives of $K$ in $G$, to extend the pattern $1_G \mapsto a, g \mapsto b$ to a legal configuration of $X^{G/K}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Infinite countable direct products $\bigoplus_{i \in \N} G_i$ of nontrivial groups $G_i$ never admit minimal sofic shifts.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}
Let $H$ be a group, and suppose $X \subset A^H$ is a subshift with at least two points. If $H \leq K \leq K * L \leq G$ for some groups $K, L, H$, then the induction of $X$ to $G$ is not minimal.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Again, we observe that we can directly take $H = K$, since inducing $X$ to $G$ is the same as first inducing it to $K$ and then to $G$. Again, we may also assume all letters from $A$ are used in points of $X$.

Let $g \in L$ be nontrivial, let $a, b \in A$ be distinct letters, and forbid from $X^{G/K}$ the pattern $1_G \mapsto a, g \mapsto a$ to obtain a subshift $Y$. We may suppose $L = \langle g \rangle$.

This new subshift is nonempty: Let $x \in X$ be arbitrary with $x_{1_G} = b$, and construct a configuration $y$ on $K * L$ by
\[ h_1 g^{n_1} h_2 g^{n_2} h_3 \cdots h_k \mapsto x_{h_k} \]
where $h_i \in K$ and $n_i \neq 0$ for all $i$. Note that such a normal form is unique. This configuration does not contain forbidden patterns of $X$, because on left cosets of $K$, we use the configuration $x$. It does not contain the new forbidden pattern since if $y_h = a$ then the normal form of $h$ ends with $h_k \neq 1_H$, and in this case the normal form of $hg$ ends with $g$, and thus $y_{hg} = x_{1_G} = b$.  As in the previous proof, we can extend $y$ to $G$ by picking coset representatives and copying it.

On the other hand, $Y \subsetneq X^{G/K}$, since clearly we can extend $\{1, g\}$ to a set of coset representatives of $K$ in $G$, to extend the pattern $1_G \mapsto a, g \mapsto a$ to a legal configuration of $X^{G/K}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Infinite countable free products $\prod_{i \in \N} G_i$ of nontrivial groups $G_i$ never admit minimal sofic shifts.
\end{corollary}

Note that the latter case covers $F_\infty$, using $G_i = \Z$ for all $i$. In particular, minimal SFTs exist on a free group if and only if it is finitely-generated. This recovers a result of Kechris and Rosendal \cite{KeRo07} (this formulation is from \cite{Do24}):

\begin{corollary}
The group $F_{\infty}$ does not admit a comeager conjugacy class of Cantor actions.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
By \cite{Do24}, it suffices to show $F_{\infty}$ does not have dense projectively isolated sofic shifts (among all subshifts over a fixed alphabet). By \cite{DoMeTs25}, projectively isolated sofic shifts are precisely the minimal sofic shifts. By the corollary above, minimal sofic shifts are singletons, so they are clearly not dense.
\end{proof}
