Unlike in the situation of non-overlapping seMCD-buckets, the algorithm will now stop under weak conditions for any $h$ including the split points, and usually much earlier than for non-overlapping seMCD-buckets or the usual plain-vanilla Monte Carlo sample sizes. Figure \ref{fig_nonstopping_regions} (b) illustrates this fact with an example: The sampling is guaranteed to stop if the seMCD-process $S_N$ reaches the white area. In this example, the gray area ends after the $M=494$ steps, so the seMCD-algorithm will have stopped for all realizations of the seMCD-process at that point.

