\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Price benchmarks are used to incorporate market price trends into contracts, but their use can create opportunities for manipulation by parties involved in the contract. This paper examines this issue using a realistic and tractable model inspired by smart contracts on blockchains like Ethereum. In our model, manipulation costs depend on two factors: the magnitude of adjustments to individual prices (variable costs) and the number of prices adjusted (fixed costs). We find that a weighted mean is the optimal benchmark when fixed costs are negligible, while the median is optimal when variable costs are negligible. In cases where both fixed and variable costs are significant, the optimal benchmark can be implemented as a trimmed mean, with the degree of trimming increasing as fixed costs become more important relative to variable costs. Furthermore, we show that the optimal weights for a mean-based benchmark are proportional to the marginal manipulation costs, whereas the median remains optimal without weighting, even when fixed costs differ across prices.



\textbf{Key Words:}  Financial benchmarks, oracles, decentralised finance. 
\newline \textbf{JEL-Classification:} G21, D45.

\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A (price) {\it benchmark} provides a summary of the price in the market at a given moment in time, quite often computed as a statistic from a sample of prices or from opinion polls.\footnote{There are other benchmarks computed out of opinion polls. This was the case of the original design of the LIBOR or the EURIBOR. They are also popular in smart contracts, see \cite{williams2019decentralized}. Augur is a familiar example, see \cite{peterson2015augur}.} They are computed for the majority of the more relevant financial markets and include popular benchmarks like the ``4pm Fix'', based on sampled prices,\footnote{The 4pm Fix'' reports the daily exchange rate of a given pair of currencies and it is computing sampling transactions around 4pm London time. For a description of the details of the ``4pm Fix'' see \cite{ber16} and \cite{eva18}.} or the original version of the LIBOR and EURIBOR, based on opinion polls.\footnote{The LIBOR, and similarly the EURIBOR, reported the interest rate at which a selection of backs could borrow in the interbank market, see \cite{hertro20} for details. } Benchmarks play a crucial role in financial markets as they allow to write contracts contingent on the evolution of prices, like derivatives, floating rate loans, portfolio evaluations... However, the notional value of these contracts has become so large that it is often the case that one of the parties may benefit from manipulating the benchmark. Indeed, there is ample evidence that this has been the case. See, for instance, the discussions in the media, \cite{schros13}, and in academic papers, \cite{eva18}.

More recently, the advent of decentralised finance (DeFi) has added a new dimension to this problem: its main building block is smart contracts\footnote{The most representative is the ethereum blockchain, which has a daily volume of transactions of around \$6 billion as of March 2024, source: \url{https://www.theblock.co/data/on-chain-metrics/ethereum}.} whose execution depends crucially on the input from some financial benchmarks, also called price oracles\footnote{\cite{angeris2020improved} studies in detail examples of price oracles that are computed using prices from transactions, for instance the Uniswap oracle, see \cite{adams2020uniswap}.} in this context.\footnote{Some times the term price oracles is interpreted in a narrow sense to refer to protocols designed to make available in a blockchain information from sources outside the blockchain. In other occasions, the term is used in a more general sense to refer to any protocol that broadcasts to the blockchain a benchmark. In this later case, we can distinguish between on and off chain oracles, see \cite{weretal22}.} 
Consider, for instance, one of the most basic financial contracts: lending. Due to the lack of enforcing courts, lending is always collateralised and an oracle is used to determine the value of the collateral. In a well documented case,\footnote{See \url{https://infotrend.com/mango-markets-madness-a-case-study-on-the-mango-markets-exploit/} and \url{https://www.sec.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2023-13} for a description.} the 
the Mango Markets exploit, a trader used \$10 million to manipulate the platform’s price oracle, inflating the value of a token used as collateral, to borrow and withdraw over \$116 million and then defaulted leading to significant platform losses.  Furthermore, lending is highly collateralised and has automatic liquidation clauses, which can be executed by any external party if the value of the collateral determined by an oracle falls below a threshold. Thus, it may be profitable to manipulate the oracle to activate liquidation.\footnote{See \cite{weretal22} for real life examples.} More generally, the development of DeFi hinges on counting with reliable oracles that can be incorporated in smart contracts. As the Financial Conduct Authority, a regulatory body of the UK Government, puts it:\footnote{See \cite{humphry2024review}.}
\begin{quote} ''‘Oracles and bridges are two of the most significant vulnerabilities that exist in the DeFi world today'' and ''any inaccuracies in the information that oracles provide can instantly impact downstream applications that rely on the oracle.'' 
\end{quote}

In this paper, we investigate how the formula used in the computation of the benchmark can be used to minimize the effect of potential manipulations. Our model highlights a key difference between traditional finance and DeFi that explains why the mean is more often used in traditional finance whereas this is the case for the median in DeFi, an observation that seems consistent with reality.\footnote{For instance, the 4pm Fix (see \url{https://www.lseg.com/en/ftse-russell}), the EURIBOR (see \url{https://www.emmi-benchmarks.eu/benchmarks/euribor/methodology/}) and the USD LIBOR (see \url{https://www.ice.com/publicdocs/USD_LIBOR_Methodology.pdf}) use a (weighted) mean, whereas popular oracles like MakerDAO or Tellor use the median, see \cite{eskandari2021sok}.} We start from the observation that manipulating the prices sampled to compute the benchmark is costly, typically because it requires trading the asset. This has been noted already by \cite{duffie2021robust} and \cite{baldauf2022principal}, although their analyses consider exclusively variable costs, i.e. the manipulation costs increases with the amount the price is manipulated. In reality, however, there may also be fixed costs that are constant in the amount the price is manipulated but grow with the number of prices manipulated. This is clearly the case when the price is determined in a decentralised exchange in a blockchain, i.e.~the typical case in DeFi. In this case, manipulating a price requires including at least one transaction in the blockchain, whose cost is independent of the amount the price is manipulated.\footnote{This cost is usually referred as the cost of gas. See \cite{perliv19} for a discussion of its main elements.} Quite remarkably, it is variable costs which may be negligible in DeFi due to the availability of certain tools like flash loans, as argued by \cite{oos21} and \cite{arora2024secplf}.\footnote{Although \cite{adams2020uniswap} argues that the existence of variable costs of manipulation may depend on the types of prices used, and that they should arise when end of block prices are used.} 

Indeed, our first main result (Proposition \ref{profixvar}) is that the optimal benchmark is the median or the mean, respectively, when either the variable costs or the fixed costs are negligible. This is intuitive. In the former case, the main trouble may come from a few prices large price increases, which are better handled with the median than with the mean. Quite the contrary, if it is fixed costs that are negligible, the mean is better to handle manipulations than the median as the median is very sensitive to the manipulation of a large proportion of prices.

Proving this result in general is technically challenging, as the problem formally corresponds to an optimal contract with multitask moral hazard. In this context, the manipulator acts as the agent, deciding how much to manipulate each of the market prices that feed into the benchmark. The analysis becomes even more complex in the presence of fixed costs, as the manipulator’s objective function is non-convex, even under linear contracts. Consequently, the manipulator’s optimal behavior cannot be fully characterized using only first-order conditions.

Our approach takes a different perspective. Consider the case with no fixed costs and a mean benchmark. Convex variable costs of manipulation result in the optimal manipulation strategy of uniformly increasing all prices in the market by the same amount. This uniform manipulation would also increase by the same amount any benchmark that satisfies basic reasonable properties. For instance, {\it symmetric benchmarks}, those equal to the point of symmetry if the distribution is symmetric, would increase the same amount if the original distribution was symmetric. However, other symmetric benchmarks may allow for alternative, less costly manipulation strategies. In this sense, the mean is optimal, as it maximizes the cost of manipulation among all symmetric benchmarks.

The case without variable costs is similar, though slightly more complex. Here, the optimal manipulation strategy of the median involves raising a sufficient mass of prices such that only half the total mass remains below the target. With careful adjustment, this minimal mass can be shifted in a way that preserves symmetry, ensuring that if the original price distribution was symmetric, the manipulated distribution remains symmetric as well. As in the previous case, this implies that the median maximizes the cost of manipulation among all symmetric benchmarks, making it optimal in this sense.

Extending the analysis beyond these extreme cases is challenging. However, in the specific scenario where the initial distribution of prices is concentrated at a single point, we can generally characterize benchmarks that induce optimal manipulations that preserve symmetry, thereby ensuring optimality among symmetric benchmarks. This turn out to be trimmed means\footnote{A trimmed mean is calculated by excluding all values below and above a given quantiles from the computation of the mean.}, where the extent of trimming increases as the fixed costs grow larger relative to the variable costs.

Finally, we study how our results extend to the case in which the prices fed into the benchmark may come from subpopulations with different observable characteristics. For instance, the sampled prices may correspond to different moments in time in which liquidity may have varied, and differences in liquidity may imply differences in the variable cost of manipulation. Similarly, different moments in time may be associated to different gas prices and thus differences in the fixed cost of manipulation.  

In this case, our results for the extreme cases of no fixed costs or no variable costs are very clear cut. In the former case, the optimal benchmark is a weighted mean with weights proportional to the marginal costs of manipulation, whereas in the latter case the standard median remains optimal. 

The most closely related papers are those of \cite{duffie2021robust}, and \cite{baldauf2022principal} that we already referred to. As we have already noted, the main difference is that they assume that manipulation has no fixed costs that grow with the number of prices manipulated. Extending their analysis to the case of fixed cost is technically challenging as the manipulator problem is no longer convex and thus we cannot work with first order conditions, as in the literature of optimal contract design, see \cite{bolton2004contract}.


Our work is also related to the literature on robust statistics, see \cite{huber} for a comprehensive review. This literature study the statistic that minimizes deviations from the true value subject to a perturbation chosen to maximize deviations. This problem, like ours, is formulated as a minimax, but the difference is that the assumptions that define the set of feasible deviations are reasonable for exogenous outliers but not for the manipulation problems that arise in financial markets. In particular, these deviations are not compatible with the existence of a fixed cost of manipulation, one of the main elements of our analysis. 

Finally, our work is also related to the fast growing literature on Decentralised Finance and in particular to the analysis of oracles and their vulnerabilities, see \cite{weretal22}. This literature, however, does not analyse the performance of the possible rules used to aggregate prices but instead focuses on an empirical investigation of the performance of the different oracles used in reality, see \cite{liu2021first}, or classify the different oracles used in practice with respect to different technical aspects of their protocols, see \cite{pasdar2021blockchain} for a comprehensive discussion. This literature is also interested in the implementation of price oracles not based on transactions but on honest reporting by trusted parties, see for instance \cite{buterin2014schellingcoin}.

\section{The Model}

\label{sec:Model}

In our investigation, we assume a population of {\it unmanipulated prices} described by a probability distribution $F$. For some results we assume that the distribution is a degenerate distribution at a price that we normalise to be zero. We refer to this case as the case of no dispersion. For the rest of the paper, we assume that $F$ has a single-peaked density $f$  symmetric around zero. A strategic agent, {\it the manipulator}, can manipulate these prices at a cost. In particular, the manipulator's strategy can be described with a conditional distribution function $G^c(\cdot|p)$, for any $p\in \real$, that describes how the prices of all the markets with unmanipulated price $p$ are shifted into a new distribution of prices. In the case of no manipulation, $G^c(\cdot|p)$ is simply a degenerate distribution at $p$. The manipulator's strategy gives rise to the {\it final distribution of prices}:
\[G(p)\equiv \int G^c(p|\tilde p) dF(\tilde p).\]

A {\it benchmark} is a unidimensional statistic computed from the final distribution of prices. In our analysis, either we require very little structure on the set of rules that may be used to compute the benchmark or restrict attention to particular families of rules that have been used in practice. In particular, we call {\it symmetric benchmarks} those that are equal to the point of symmetry in the case of a symmetric distribution of prices. Note that this definition does not impose any structure on distributions that are not symmetric. We define the $\tau$-trimmed mean, for $\tau \in [0, 0.5]$, as the average calculated over the modified distribution obtained by truncating the original distribution at the $\tau$ and $1-\tau$ quantiles. Clearly, $\tau=0$ and $\tau=1/2$, renders the $\tau-$trimmed mean equal to the standard mean and the median, respectively. Clearly, the $\tau$-trimmed means are symmetric benchmarks.

As a realistic example, we can think of the distribution of prices as the marginal prices offered by a set of automated market makers (AMM) at different moments in time. This marginal prices can be manipulated by submitting trade orders to the AMM whose only purpose is to upset the computed benchmark. Thus, the final prices correspond to the marginal prices displayed by the AMM's and used in the computation of the benchmark and the unmanipulated prices refer to the counterfactual of what could have been the marginal prices if there was no manipulation. Alternatively, we could have assumed that the benchmark is computed out of a sample of transaction prices and manipulation is done by submitting additional trades.

Manipulating a price is costly. In particular, the cost of a manipulating strategy $G^c(\cdot|p)$ is equal to:
\[\int \int_{{\tilde p\not =p}} \left( c(\tilde p-p) +k \right) dG(\tilde p|p) dF(p),\] where $c$ is a continuous strictly convex function, equal to zero at zero and symmetric around zero, and $k\geq 0$. Thus, $c$ is the {\it variable cost} of manipulation and $k$ the {\it fixed cost} of manipulation. The cost of manipulation is explained in that manipulating a price requires trading the asset. The variable cost arises because greater manipulation requieres a larger order which may imply larger costs\footnote{Both \cite{duffie2021robust}, and \cite{baldauf2022principal} provide examples of why the variable cost is increasing and convex.} and the fixed cost because including a trader order has a cost independently of the size of the trade order. For instance, in the context of blockchain, manipulating a price just before it is fed to the benchmark requires paying the miner the corresponding price of gas, which may be substantial. 

The symmetry of the distribution of unmanipulated prices means that any symmetric benchmark is equal to zero, and thus the {\it  bias} of the benchmark due to manipulation is the absolute value of the benchmark computed for the final distribution of prices.

\section{Optimal Benchmarks}
\label{secOpt}

The criterium we use to evaluate a benchmark is the minimum cost of manipulation necessary to implement a certain price $P$, the greater this minimum cost is, the better the benchmark. This is consistent with the example of collateralised loans that we provide in the introduction. A manipulator minimizes the cost to reach the price at which the liquidation clause is activated, whereas the benchmark designer maximizes this cost to make this manipulation less attractive. This is also a first step to study alternative objective functions for the manipulator. For instance, for the case in which the manipulator's objective is to maximize the price, the optimal benchmark maximizes the marginal cost of raising it, which under some regularity conditions coincides with the benchmark that maximizes the total cost of raising the benchmark.

\begin{definition}$\,$
\begin{itemize}
\item A benchmark is optimal at price $P$ if it is the symmetric benchmark that maximizes the minimum cost of manipulating the benchmark up to $P$. A benchmark is optimal if it is optimal at any price $P$.
\item A benchmark dominates another benchmark at price $P$ if the former has a greater minimum cost of its manipulation to $P$ than the latter. A benchmark dominates another benchmark if it dominates the latter for any $P$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

In our analysis, we shall build on an intuitive property.

\begin{proposition}
A benchmark is optimal at $P$ if its optimal manipulation strategy induces a final distribution of prices symmetric around $P$.
\label{prosym}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Since the manipulation strategy of the proposition induces a symmetric final distribution of prices, it not only implements $P$ for the proposed benchmark but also for any symmetric benchmark. This manipulation strategy is optimal for the proposed benchmark, but other symmetric benchmarks manipulators may have less costly manipulation strategies that also implement $P$, which implies the proposition. 
\end{proof}

Our first main result covers the case of no fixed cost or no variable costs.

\begin{proposition}
The mean is an optimal benchmark in the case of no fixed cost, i.e. $k=0$, whereas the median is an optimal benchmark in the case of no variable costs, i.e. $c=0$.
\label{profixvar}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
We start with the case $k=0$ and an arbitrary price $P>0$ (the analysis of negative prices is symmetric). The convexity of $c$ and the lack of fixed costs means that to minimize the cost of raising the mean to $P$ the manipulator should raise all prices to $P$. This shifts the whole distribution up the same amount and thus give rise to a symmetric distribution. Thus, the first result follows from the application of Proposition \ref{prosym}. 

Consider next the case $c=0$ and an arbitrary price $P>0$. The less costly way of raising the median to $P$ is to shift the mass between $0$ and $P$ to $P$. The total mass moved is $F(P)-1/2$, which has a cost of $k (F(P)-1/2)$. Note that the same increase



 in the median can be obtained at the same cost with any shift that moves a mass of exactly  $F(P)-1/2$ prices from strictly below $P$ to weakly above $P$. To conclude the proof, we show that there exists a manipulator strategy that achieves this and at the same time induces a distribution of manipulated prices symmetric around $P$.  

The manipulation strategy is as follows. Let $\tilde g(y)\equiv \frac{f(y)+f(y-2P)}{2}$, $\alpha (y)\equiv \frac{\tilde g(y)}{f(y)}$ and $A(y)\equiv \frac{\int_P^\infty \frac{f(z-2P)-f(z)}{2} dz }{1-\alpha(y)} $ for $y<P$. Note that the symmetry around 0 and single peakness of $f$ implies that $f(y)>f(y-2P)$ for $y<P$, and thus that $\alpha(y)\in (0,1)$. Then, the conditional distribution that describes the manipulation $G^c(\cdot|y)$ is a degenerate distribution at $y$ for any $y\geq P$, and a distribution with a point mass $\alpha (y)$ at $y$ and a density $\frac{\tilde g(x)-f(x)}{A(y)}$ for $x\in [P,\infty)$. The total mass shifted is equal to:
\begin{eqnarray*}
 \int_{-\infty}^P \int_P^{\infty}  \frac{\tilde g(x)-f(x)}{A(y)} dx f(y) dy &=& \int_{-\infty}^P(1-\alpha(y))  \frac{\int_P^{\infty} \tilde g(x)-f(x)}{ \int_P^\infty \frac{f(z-2P)-f(z)}{2} dz } dx f(y) dy\\
 &=&\int_{-\infty}^P(1-\alpha(y)) f(y) dy\\
 &=&\int_{-\infty}^P\frac{f(y)-f(y-2P)}{2} dy\\
 &=&\frac{F(P)-F(-P)}{2}\\
&=&F(P)-\frac{1}{2}, 
 \end{eqnarray*}
where we use the definition of $A(y)$ in the first step, the definition of $\tilde g(x)$ in the second, the definition of $\alpha(y)$ and $\tilde g(y)$ in the third, and the symmetry of $f$ with respect to zero in the last step. To show that the induced distribution of manipulated prices is symmetric around $P$, we just show that its density is $\tilde g$, which satisfies $\tilde g(P-x)=\tilde g(P+x)$ as desired. For $x<P$, this density is equal to $\alpha(x)f(x)=\tilde g(x)$ as desired, and for $x\geq P$, it is equal to:
\begin{eqnarray*}
f(x)+ \int_{-\infty}^P   \frac{\tilde g(x)-f(x)}{A(y)} dx f(y) dy &=& f(x)+ \left( \tilde g(x)-f(x)\right)    \frac{\int_{-\infty}^P\left( 1-\alpha(y)\right) f(y) dy }{\int_P^\infty \frac{f(z-2P)-f(z)}{2} dz}\\
 &=& f(x)+ \left( \tilde g(x)-f(x)\right)    \frac{\int_{-\infty}^P\left(  f(y)-f(y-2P)\right) dy }{\int_P^\infty\left(  f(z-2P)-f(z) \right)dz}\\
  &=& f(x)+ \left( \tilde g(x)-f(x)\right)    \frac{\int_{-\infty}^P\left(  f(y)-f(y-2P)\right) dy }{\int_{-\infty}^P \left(  f(-y)-f(2P-y) \right)dy}\\
  &=& \tilde g(x),
 \end{eqnarray*}
 where in the first step, we use the definition of $A(y)$; in the second step, the definition of $\alpha(y)$ and $\tilde g(y)$; in the third the change of variable $y=2P-z$; and in the fourth, the symmetry of $f$ with respect to zero.
\end{proof}

The general case in which we allow for both fixed and variable costs is substantially more complex in general. To see why note that it corresponds to a multitask moral hazard problem in which the agent has non convex preferences even in the case of linear contracts. Still, there are two particular cases in which we can easily generalise Proposition \ref{profixvar} and still apply Proposition \ref{prosym}.

Let $\overline c(q)\equiv\frac{k+c(q)}{|q|}$ be the average cost of manipulating a single price the amount $q$, and $\delta_{min}$ denote the minimum of the function $\overline c(q)$ for $q\in [0,\infty)$. Strict convexity means that there is a unique $\delta_{min}\in [0,\infty)$ unless $\overline c$ is always decreasing. In the latter case, we fix $\delta_{min}=\infty$. 

\begin{proposition}
The mean is an optimal benchmark if $\delta_{min} \leq |P|$ or $\delta_{min}=|2P|$.
\label{prodP}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} Consider the case $P>0$ for simplicity. The strict convexity of the variable cost function implies that the optimal manipulation of the mean is to raise the price in all the markets manipulated by the same amount. If we denote this amount by $\delta$ and by $\Delta$ the measure of manipulated prices, the optimal values solve:
\[ \left\{ \begin{array}{cl} \min & \Delta(k+c(\delta))\\ {\scriptstyle \delta\in \real_+,\Delta \in [0,1]}\\ \mbox{s.t.} & \delta \Delta=P .\end{array} \right\} = \min_{\delta \in (P, \infty)} P \cdot\overline c(\delta),\]
whose solution when $\delta_{min} \leq P$ is $\delta=P$ and $\Delta=1$, and when $\delta_{min} = 2 P$ is $\delta=\delta_{min} $ and $\Delta=1/2$. In both cases, this manipulative strategy can give rise to a distribution of manipulated prices symmetric around $P$. This is in the former case because the whole distribution is shifted the same amount and the original distribution is symmetric and in the latter case because we can just shift the upper half of the distribution of prices. Thus, we can apply Proposition \ref{prosym} as desired.
\end{proof}

In both cases in the proposition the mean always has an optimal manipulation of prices that preserves the symmetry of the distribution. This is not generally the case if either $\delta_{min}\in (P,2P)$ or $\delta>2P$. This is easy to see in the case of no dispersion of unmanipulated prices. The strict convexity of the variable costs implies that the optimal manipulation of the mean is to increase all manipulated prices the same amount. If $\delta_{min}\geq P$, this is to raise $\delta_{min}$ a measure of $p/\delta_{min}$ of prices. Thus, $\delta_{min}\in (P,2P)$ and $\delta>2P$ imply that the measure of manipulated prices is $\Delta\in (1/2,1)$ and $\Delta<1/2$, respectively. This produces a distribution of manipulated prices with two atoms, one at 0 and one at $\delta_{min}$ that it is not symmetric. The next proposition characterizes the optimal benchmark in the latter case.\footnote{Extending this result to more general distributions is complex. Even in one restricts to relatively simple rules used in reality like the $\tau-$trimmed and $\tau-$Winsorized means the characterization of the optimal manipulative strategy is complex because the manipulator's problem is a non separable and non convex functional optimization problem. }

\begin{proposition}
\label{prod2P}
Suppose a distribution of unmanipulated prices with no dispersion and $\delta_{min}>2P$. The optimal benchmark is the $\tau-$trimmed mean with quantile:
\[\tau^*\equiv \frac{1}{2} \left( 1- \frac{c'(2P)}{\overline c(2P)} \right)\in (0,1/2).\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Consider the optimal manipulation strategy of a $\tau$-trimmed mean. Since the cost of manipulation is convex and all markets are identical the optimal manipulation is to raise the same amount, say $\delta$, the prices of a measure of markets, say $\Delta$. To make the price increase up to $P$, we require that $\frac{\delta (\Delta-\tau)}{1-2\tau}=P$ and $\Delta \in (\tau,1-\tau]$. The optimal values of $\delta$ and $\Delta$ solve:
\begin{eqnarray*}
\min_{\delta,\Delta} & \Delta \left( k+c(\delta)\right)\\
\mbox{s.t.} & \frac{\delta (\Delta-\tau)}{1-2\tau}=P
\\& \Delta \in (\tau,1-\tau] .
\end{eqnarray*}
The first equality means that $\Delta=\tau + (1-2\tau)\frac{P}{\delta}$, thus the bounds of $\Delta$ means that $\delta\geq P$ and the former minimization problem is equivalent to:
\begin{equation}
\label{eqMIN}
\min_{\delta \geq P} \left\{ P (1-2\tau) \left( \frac{ k+c(\delta)}{\delta} \right)+ \tau \left( k+c(\delta)\right) \right\}.
\end{equation}

The derivative of the objective function with respect to $\delta$ is equal to:
\begin{equation}
 -P (1-2\tau) \frac{\overline c(\delta) -c'(\delta)}{\delta}   + \tau  c'(\delta).
\label{eqFOC}
\end{equation}

The strict convexity\footnote{Recall from Intermediate Microeconomics that a convex cost function has a U-Shape average cost function with minimum at the point at which marginal and average cost cross, being the former smaller than the latter before the crossing point and the former larger than the latter otherwise.} of $c$ implies that for $\delta \in [0,\delta_{min})$, $\overline c(\delta)-c'(\delta)>0$ and decreasing, and thus \eqref{eqFOC} is convex in $\delta \in [0,\delta_{min})$. Similarly, for $\delta>\delta_{min}$, $\overline c(\delta)<c'(\delta)$, which together with $c'(\delta)\geq 0$ means that \eqref{eqFOC} is positive in $[\delta_{min},\infty)$. Besides, \eqref{eqFOC} tends to infinity as $\delta$ tends to zero. Thus, there exists a unique $\delta^*(\tau)\in (0,\delta_{min})$ such that \eqref{eqFOC} is negative for $\delta \in (0,\delta^*(\tau))$ and positive for $\delta \in (\delta^*(\tau)),\infty)$. The solution to \eqref{eqMIN} is thus $\max\{P,\delta^*(\tau)\}$.

Next note that $\delta^*(\tau^*)=2P$ since replacing $\tau$ by $\tau^*$ and $\delta$ by $2P$ in \eqref{eqFOC} makes it equal to zero. Consequently, the optimal $\delta$ for $\tau^*$ is $2P$, which corresponds to an optimal $\Delta=1/2$. This manipulative strategy gives rise to a distribution of manipulated prices symmetric around $P$. Thus, the proposition follows from applying Proposition \ref{prosym}. 
\end{proof}

The essence of the proof is to note that convexity means that the optimal manipulation raises the prices the same amount and that the proposed $\tau$ makes it optimal to raise them to $2P$. Consequently, just half the prices are raised, which means we can apply Proposition \ref{prosym}. This does not work in the case $\delta_{min}\in (P,2P)$ as the optimal manipulation strategy for any trimmed mean does not raise the prices above $\delta_{min}$, and thus, the mass of prices raised is strictly less than half, which means that the final distribution is not symmetric. Still, one can prove that the mean does better than any other trimmed mean which is consistent with the results in Propositions \ref{prodP} and \ref{prod2P} (note that $\delta_{min}=2P$ implies that $\tau^*=0$).

\begin{proposition} If $\delta_{min} \in (P,2P)$, the untrimmed mean dominates at $P$ any trimmed mean when the  unmanipulated prices have no dispersion. 
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
In this proof, we use the arguments in the proof of Proposition \ref{prod2P} to conclude that the optimal manipulation of a $\tau-$trimmed mean consists of raising an amount $\delta^* \in [P,\delta_{min}]$ a measure of prices $\Delta=\tau + (1-2\tau)\frac{P}{\delta}$, where $\delta^*$ is the $\delta$ that solves \eqref{eqMIN}. Besides, the objective function of \eqref{eqMIN} evaluated at $\delta^*$ is equal to the cost of manipulating the $\tau-$trimmed mean. If $\delta^*=P$, it is direct to show that this cost is decreasing in $\tau$, and if $\delta^*>P$, we can use that $\delta^*\leq \delta_{min}$, as explained below \eqref{eqFOC}, and apply  the envelope theorem to show that the derivative of the cost of manipulation with respect to $\tau$ is equal to:
\[ -\frac{(k+c(\delta^*)}{\delta^*})(2P-\delta^*)=-\frac{(k+c(\delta^*)}{\delta^*})(2P-\delta_{min})<0,\]
as desired.
\end{proof}

Although our focus on the case of no dispersion of prices was for tractability reasons, note that the prevalence of extensive arbitrage facilitated by chatbots, as discussed in \cite{daia20flash}, implies that prices often gravitate close to the mean/median, which makes it a reasonable assumption in the context of Defi.

