\section{Benchmarks Based on Data from Heterogenous Markets}

In this section, we consider the case in which the distribution of prices comes from different subpopulations that may differ in their manipulation costs. This difference may arise, for instance, because the benchmark is computed using prices at different moments in time and manipulation costs may vary along time. A natural example is when liquidity varies along time. In this case, lower liquidity means that the same amount of manipulation requires of a smaller order and thus smaller variable costs. Another example in the context of the blockchain is when gas prices fluctuate with time. In this case, lower gas prices correspond to lower fixed costs.

We also allow for the possibility of heterogeneity across subpopulations in the distribution of the unmanipulated prices. Formally, we assume a countable set of subpopulations $I$, where subpopulation $i\in I$ has mass $\mu_i$ and it is described by a probability distribution $F_i$, with density $f_i$ and single peaked. We assume that the distribution $F=\sum_i \mu_i F_i$ is symmetric around zero.

The manipulator strategy is described by a family, one per subpopulation, of conditional distribution functions $\{G^c_i(\cdot|p)\}_{i\in I}$, that describe how much each price in each subpopulation is shifted by the manipulator. The manipulator strategy gives rise to a final distribution of prices for each population $i$:
\[G_i(\tilde p)\equiv \int G^c_i(\tilde p| p)\,  \mu_i\,  F_i\left(  d p \right).\]

The cost of a manipulating strategy $\{G^c_i(\cdot|p)\}_{i\in I}$ is equal to:
\[\sum_{i\in I} \int \int_{{\tilde p\not =p}} \left( \alpha_i c(\tilde p-p) +k_i \right) \,G_i(d \tilde p|p)\,  \mu_i\,  F\left( d p\right),\] where the $c_i$'s are continuous convex function, equal to zero at zero and symmetric around zero and  $k_i\geq 0$. Thus, the $c_i$'s are the {\it variable cost} of manipulation and the $k_i$'s the {\it fixed costs} of manipulation. 

A benchmark in this section is again a unidimensional statistic that summarizes the distribution of prices. The novelty in this section is that we allow the benchmark to depend on the whole vector of distributions of final prices of all subpopulations, $(G_i(p))_{i\in I}$, rather than in the population distribution $G$. Thus, we can measure the bias created by manipulation as deviations from zero.

Our main result in this section refers to the case of either no fixed cost or no variable costs.

\begin{proposition} $\,$
\label{proweighted}
\begin{itemize}
\item The optimal benchmark at price\footnote{As it is easy, the proposed benchmark is optimal at any price if the variable costs of each population are such that $c_i(x)=\beta_i c(x)$ for some function $c$.} $P$ in the case of no fixed cost, i.e. $k_i=0$ for all $i\in I$, is a weighted mean of prices where each price in subpopulation $i$ has a weight $\frac{c'_i(P)}{\sum_j c'_j(P) }$.
\item The optimal benchmark in the case of no variable costs, i.e. $c_i(\cdot)=0$ for all $i\in I$, is the median.
\end{itemize}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} Suppose without loss that $P>0$. 
To prove the first item, we compute the optimal manipulation strategy of the proposed benchmark. Since the costs are convex, it is optimal to raise exactly the same amount all the prices that belong to the same subpopulation. Denote by $\delta_i$ the amount by which the prices in subpopulation $i$ are raised. The optimal $\delta_i$'s solve:
\begin{eqnarray*}
\min_{\{\delta_i\}_{i\in I}} & \sum_i c_i(\delta_i) \mu_i \\
\mbox{s.t.} & \sum_i \frac{c'_i(P)}{\sum_j c'_j(P)\mu_j} \mu_i \delta_i =P,
\end{eqnarray*}
where $\mu_i$ is the mass of prices in subpopulation $i$. It is standard to check that $\delta_i=P$, for all $i\in I$, solves the first order conditions of the corresponding Lagrangian. This means manipulating all the prices an amount $P$. Since the original subpopulations were symmetric and distributed symmetrically around the axis of symmetry of the population, so is the final distribution of prices. Consequently, the proposed manipulation will raise to $P$ any symmetric benchmark, and thus the proposed benchmark is optimal.

The proof of the second item is identical to the proof of Proposition \ref{profixvar} for $c=0$ as the only feature of the cost function required in that proof is that there are no variable costs.
\end{proof}

Although restricting attention to symmetric benchmarks seems natural, it may be unnecessarily restrictive in the case of heterogeneous populations. For instance, neither the mean of the medians of the subpopulations nor the median of the medians of subpopulations are symmetric. To see why, suppose a population distributed uniformly in $[-1,1]$ and composed of three uniformly distributed subpopulations, one over $[-1,.4]$, another one over $[.4,.8]$ and the last one over $[.8,1]$. Any symmetric benchmark must be equal to zero, whereas the mean of the medians and the median of the medians are equal to 0.6. To allow for the latter benchmarks, we shall consider next the set of {\it doubly symmetric benchmarks}, these are benchmarks that are equal to the point of symmetry of the distribution whenever all the subpopulations are symmetric, and the set of subpopulations is also symmetric around the point of symmetry of the population. Formally, we say that a set of subpopulations $(G_i)_{i\in I}$ is symmetric around $X$ if there exists a function $y:I\rightarrow I$ such that for any $x\in \real$ $\mu_i G'_i(x)=\mu_j G'_{y(i)} (X-x)$. A simple example is when all the distributions of the subpopulations are symmetric around the same value $X$, i.e. $G'_i(x)=G'_i(X-x)$. In this case, we can take $y(i)=i$ for all $i\in I$. Under the assumption that each of the subpopulations $F_i$ is symmetric and that the set of subpopulations $(F_i)_{i\in I}$ is also symmetric, any double symmetric benchmark is equal to zero for the unmanipulated prices. The following definition is thus meaningful under this assumption.

\begin{definition}
A benchmark is optimal$^*$ at price $P$ if it is the doubly symmetric benchmark that maximizes the minimum cost of manipulating the benchmark up to $P$. A benchmark is optimal$^*$ if it is optimal at any price $P$.
\end{definition}

Characterizing optimal$^*$ benchmarks, however, requires additional assumptions in the case of no variable costs.

\begin{proposition} $\,$
\begin{itemize}
\item If there are no fixed cost, i.e. $k_i=0$ for all $i\in I$, each of the subpopulations $F_i$ is symmetric and the set of subpopulations $(F_i)_{i\in I}$ is also symmetric, then the optimal$^*$ benchmark at price $P$ is a weighted mean of prices where each price in subpopulation $i$ has a weight $\frac{c'_i(P)}{\sum_j c'_j(P) }$.
\item If there are no variable costs, i.e. $c_i=0$ for all $i\in I$, and each of the subpopulations $F_i$ is symmetric around $0$, then the optimal$^*$ benchmark is the median.
\end{itemize}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The proof of the first item follows from the proof of the first item of Proposition \ref{proweighted} and the observation that the distributions of manipulated prices are just a shift of the distributions of unmanipulated prices. The proof of the second item is also similar to the proof of Proposition \ref{profixvar} for $c=0$. We just need to apply the argument for each of the subpopulations and note that the resulting subpopulations of distributions of manipulated prices are all symmetric around $P$.
\end{proof}


\section{Conclusion}

This paper investigates how the design of price benchmarks can mitigate the risks of manipulation, with a particular focus on the unique challenges posed by decentralized finance (DeFi). We demonstrate that the choice of benchmark formula—mean, median, or trimmed mean—plays a critical role in balancing the costs of manipulation. Our results highlight that while the mean is optimal when variable costs dominate, the median is preferable when fixed costs are significant. In intermediate cases, trimmed means emerge as optimal, with the degree of trimming reflecting the relative importance of fixed costs.

By formalizing the trade-offs between fixed and variable manipulation costs, our work offers a framework to enhance benchmark robustness in both traditional finance and DeFi. Our findings are particularly relevant for the development of DeFi, since reliable price benchmarks are essential to the trustworthiness of smart contracts and the stability of DeFi systems.

A key direction for future research is the joint optimization of sampling strategies and benchmark formulas. While this paper focuses on the formula used to aggregate sampled prices, the sampling process itself is equally critical, particularly in decentralized exchanges where liquidity and transaction timing can vary widely. Exploring how sampling protocols interact with benchmark formulas could yield further insights into designing benchmarks that are both manipulation-resistant and statistically efficient.





