\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

With the rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) \cite{1brown2020language,2touvron2023llama}, LLM-based agents have been increasingly integrated into a wide range of real-world applications, from virtual assistants and language tutors to code generation and scientific research tools \cite{3guo2024large}. Beyond single-agent use, recent developments have enabled the construction of multi-agent systems powered by LLMs (LLM-MAS) \cite{4hong2023metagpt}, where multiple LLM-driven agents collaborate to solve complex tasks that require specialization, planning, and coordination \cite{5qian2023communicative}. While such systems unlock promising capabilities, the increasing complexity of inter-agent communication has also raised concerns about coordination failures, degraded outcomes, and unexpected system-wide behaviors \cite{6liu2019emergent}.

These concerns have sparked growing interest in the safety of LLM-based multi-agent systems \cite{7hendrycks2020aligning}. Much of the current research focuses on explicit adversarial behaviors, such as prompt injection \cite{8chao2023jailbreaking}, jailbreak instructions \cite{9yi2024jailbreak}, and backdoor attacks \cite{10wang2024badagent}, where individual agents produce harmful or misleading outputs. These attacks typically rely on direct anomalies and can often be mitigated through output-level filtering or behavioral constraints \cite{11ouyang2022training}. However, failures in LLM-MAS may also arise from more subtle communication dynamics \cite{du2023improving}, where agents influence one another in ways that gradually degrade system performance. While these issues have received less attention, they indicate the need to move beyond surface-level correctness when evaluating LLM-based multi-agent systems safety \cite{13wooldridge2009introduction}.
\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=1.02\linewidth]{figs/illustration/scenario1.png}
\caption{A malicious agent subtly shifts the discussion from Q1 to Q2, causing the group to converge on an subtask.}
\label{fig:scenario}
\end{figure}

Cemri et al. have reported that certain forms of indirect interaction can lead to failures in multi-agent LLM systems \cite{cemri2025multi}. These issues are often regarded as unintentional coordination breakdowns rather than the result of deliberate interference. However, limited investigation has been given to how such behaviors could be exploited as intentional attack strategies.This raises a critical concerns: if an agent intentionally disrupts group reasoning while remaining fluent and consistent with its assigned role, would existing systems be able to detect it? As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:scenario}, a malicious agent can gradually influence the direction of discussion by reframing key questions or reinforcing misleading premises, causing the group to reach incorrect conclusions without violating explicit rules. This scenario indicates that LLM-MAS may be vulnerable not only to explicit errors but also to agents whose harmful intent is concealed by superficially cooperative behavior. This observation reveals a significant and underexplored dimension of LLM-MAS safety.

\noindent \textbf{Research Questions.} To fill the above gaps, we conduct the first systematic study on the impact, structures and detectability of intention-hiding malicious agents in LLM-MAS. Specifically, this paper aims to answer the following three research questions.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{RQ1}: Do intention-hiding agents significantly disrupt multi-agent collaboration?
    \item \textbf{RQ2}: Are such attacks consistently effective across different communication structures?
    \item \textbf{RQ3}: Can intention-hiding behavior be effectively detected?
\end{itemize}

\noindent \textbf{Our Work.} To address \textbf{RQ1}, we examine whether intention-hiding malicious agents can disrupt the overall performance of LLM-based multi-agent systems. We conduct experiments across six benchmark datasets, including MMLU, MMLU-Pro, HumanEval, GSM8K, Arithmetic, and Biographies. Within these settings, malicious agents are introduced using prompt injection with carefully designed strategies that preserve surface-level fluency and remain consistent with their assigned roles. The results show that, across tasks and structures, the presence of such agents consistently leads to degraded performance metrics, including lower task success rates, prolonged response times, and increased token consumption in group responses. 

To answer \textbf{RQ2}, we assess whether intention-hiding attacks maintain their effectiveness across different communication structures. We construct four attack paradigms: suboptimal fixation, reframing misalignment, fake injection, and execution delay. 
% Each targets a distinct aspect of collaborative influence while preserving role compliance and linguistic plausibility. 
These attacks are deployed in multi-agent systems configured under centralized, decentralized, and layered communication structures. Experimental results across all six datasets indicate that each attack paradigm can successfully disrupt task completion under all three structures, though the degree of disruption varies depending on the system's communication structure. 

To investigate \textbf{RQ3}, we propose a psychologically grounded detection framework \sectionstyleword{AgentXposed}, which combines structured personality assessment with interaction-level behavior monitoring. The framework integrates the HEXACO personality model with the Reid interrogation technique to construct progressive questionnaires tailored to multi-turn interactions and behaviorally consistent agent profiles. To evaluate its effectiveness, we compare HEXACO with alternative personality profiling methods, including the Myers–Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI) and the Big Five Personality Traits. Results show that HEXACO yields superior detection performance.

To further assess the challenge of detecting intention-hiding behaviors, we compare detection rates on our attacks with two baseline paradigms: Incorrect Fact Injection and Psysafe. \sectionstyleword{AgentXposed} demonstrates high accuracy across all six types of adversarial agents. Its detection rate for the four intention-hiding attacks is slightly lower than for the two baselines.  demonstrates both the concealment and disruptive potential of our proposed intention-hiding attack paradigms.

\noindent \textbf{Contributions.} Above all, our contributions can be summarized as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item We conduct the first unified study of intention-hiding attacks in LLM-based multi-agent systems, revealing their capacity to degrade collaboration while maintaining role-consistent and fluent outputs.
    \item We design four representative intention-hiding attack paradigms and evaluate their effectiveness across centralized, decentralized, and layered communication structures on six benchmark datasets.
    \item We develop a psychologically grounded detection framework, \sectionstyleword{AgentXposed}, based on the HEXACO personality model and the Reid Technique. We demonstrate that HEXACO outperforms MBTI and the Big Five Personality Traits in detecting intention-hiding agents, and that \sectionstyleword{AgentXposed} achieves strong performance across all attack types.
\end{itemize}

