\section{Background And Related Work}

\subsection{Multi-Agent Collaboration with LLM}
LLM-based multi-agent systems have been studied under three main communication structures: centralized, decentralized, and layered \cite{01tran2025multi}. In centralized systems, a coordinator agent directs others, as in ChatDev, where one agent assigns tasks to sub-agents and aggregates outputs \cite{03qian2023chatdev}. This architecture simplifies control but creates a single point of failure. In contrast, decentralized systems operate through peer-to-peer communication, promoting robustness but increasing coordination complexity. Examples include Generative Agents, where agents interact freely without a global controller \cite{04park2023generative}. Layered systems introduce hierarchy: agents at higher levels oversee subtasks performed by lower-level agents. CAMEL exemplifies this via structured role-based collaboration between a user and assistant agent \cite{05li2023camel}. Surveys classify these structures and emphasize their influence on coordination dynamics and vulnerability to adversarial manipulation \cite{06li2024survey,07guo2024large}. Our work builds on this by analyzing how communication structure affects the expression and detectability of malicious behavior in LLM-MAS.

\subsection{Malicious Agent Modeling and Attacks}
While LLM-MAS are designed for cooperation, agents can behave adversarially. Huang et al. \cite{08huang2024resilience} note that some agents generate misleading outputs that remain undetected by peers. Attacks can target communication itself, such as Agent-in-the-Middle attacks that modify inter-agent messages \cite{09he2025red},  or propagate via prompt-level infections like Prompt Infection \cite{001lee2024prompt}, where harmful prompts spread contextually between agents. Evil Geniuses shows coordinated jailbreaks that bypass safety through role-based manipulation \cite{002tian2023evil},  while DemonAgent \cite{003zhu2025demonagent} and BLAST \cite{004yu2025blast} demonstrate stealthy prompt fragmentation and latent backdoor triggering. These techniques induce misalignment across communication structures while concealing intent. Our work complements these efforts by formalizing intention-hiding attacks and assessing their effects across different MAS configurations.

\subsection{Malicious Agent Detection}
To mitigate threats in LLM-MAS, recent studies propose detecting malicious agents through monitoring, profiling, and dialogue validation. Conventional LLM moderators often miss distributed malicious behavior,  prompting methods based on personality profiling and dynamic oversight \cite{005gao2024large}. Systems like GuardAgent \cite{007xiang2024guardagent} and AgentGuard \cite{006chen2025agentguard} introduce observer agents that intercept unsafe actions in real-time. In decentralized setups, dedicated watchdog agents are deployed. Some works apply multi-agent consensus, where validators or referee agents verify others’ outputs to improve safety. Graph-based methods like G-Safeguar77d encode dialogue as a graph and apply GNNs to flag abnormal influence patterns \cite{008wang2025g,009hua2024trustagent}. These strategies inspire our detection framework, which targets intention-hiding agents under varied communication structures and integrates psychological screening for early identification.

\section{Methodology}
In this section, we introduce our methodology for modeling and detecting intention-hiding malicious agents in LLM-based multi-agent systems. 
We begin with an overview of the system architecture,  followed by a specific description of each component.

\subsection{Overview}

Figure~\ref{fig:overview} illustrates the architecture of our proposed system, which consists of three core components: a multi-agent collaboration environment, a set of intention-hiding attack paradigms, and a psychologically grounded detection module.

We begin by configuring a collaborative environment composed of LLM-based agents assigned different roles and prompts. Agents interact through one of three canonical communication structures: \textit{centralized}, \textit{decentralized}, and \textit{layered}. Each of these defines the flow of messages, the coordination of decisions, and the propagation of influence. These structures serve as experimental conditions under which we analyze the impact and detectability of covert attacks.

Building on this foundation, we define four paradigms of intention-hiding adversarial behavior: \textit{suboptimal fixation}, \textit{reframing misalignment}, \textit{fake injection}, and \textit{execution delay}. These strategies allow a malicious agent to behave fluently and contextually while subtly undermining group performance or distorting reasoning processes. All behaviors are carefully designed to remain linguistically and role-consistent, thereby avoiding detection through standard anomaly signals.

To detect such threats, we propose \sectionstyleword{AgentXposed}, a detection framework that integrates structured personality profiling with multi-stage behavioral monitoring. It combines the HEXACO personality model and the Reid interrogation technique to generate progressive, task-sensitive questionnaires. In parallel, it tracks agent behavior over time to identify anomalous influence patterns. This dual-layer mechanism enables the system to flag covert adversaries whose manipulations are otherwise masked by surface-level cooperation.

\subsection{Intention-Hiding Attack}

In this subsection, we first introduce the communication structures, which serve as the structural foundation for the attacks. We then present four intention-hiding attack paradigms that model covert disruption strategies of malicious agents. Finally, we describe the task-specific scenarios in which these attacks are instantiated.

\noindent \textbf{Communication Structures} We construct a unified multi-agent environment in which all agents are instantiated from the same pre-trained large language model and assigned distinct roles via prompt engineering. Some agents are also equipped with access to external tools.ols. Each agent independently generates outputs in multi-turn conversations, collaborating to solve complex tasks.

To reflect the diversity of real-world coordination mechanisms, we implement three canonical communication topologies: \textit{centralized}, \textit{decentralized}, and \textit{layered}. 

\paragraph{Centralized} In the centralized setting, a single coordinator agent is responsible for task decomposition and result aggregation. For example, a central planner may first analyze the task, delegate subtasks to domain-specific experts, and then route the responses to a verifier for final check-up. 

\paragraph{Decentralized} In the decentralized structure, all agents communicate in a peer-to-peer manner. A typical scenario involves multi-agent discussion, where each agent observes others’ messages, performs independent analysis, and contributes to consensus formation through voting. 

\paragraph{Layered} The layered structure introduces hierarchical control: Higher-level agents oversee global planning and integration, while lower-level agents handle execution of specialized subtasks.

\noindent \textbf{Attack Paradigms} 
We design four paradigms of intention-hiding attacks, each simulating a distinct form of covert disruption that can undermine multi-agent collaboration while remaining consistent with role expectations and dialogue norms. 

\setcounter{paragraph}{0}
\paragraph {Suboptimal Fixation} This paradigm targets the decision-making process by promoting solutions that are plausible but intentionally inferior. Let $O^*$ denote the optimal group outcome for a given task, and let $O'$ denote a suboptimal yet defensible alternative such that $O' \neq O^*$ but $O'$ remains logically consistent with the task constraints. The malicious agent persistently advocates for $O'$ by emphasizing heuristics such as safety, simplicity, or empirical conservatism. 

Importantly, $O'$ is selected such that it cannot be trivially refuted, allowing the agent to maintain apparent rationality. Fixation is implemented through consistent rhetorical framing across multiple turns, including reasserting trade-offs, downplaying alternatives, or selectively citing incomplete evidence. Since the agent’s reasoning aligns with risk-averse or conservative behavior, it appears to be helpful rather than adversarial. However, the cumulative effect of its influence is to steer the group away from $O^*$ towards the strategically preferred $O'$, thereby degrading overall performance while evading detection.

\paragraph {Reframing Misalignment}
This paradigm manipulates the semantic framing of the task to covertly alter the direction of group reasoning. Let $Q$ denote the original problem formulation and $Q'$ a reframed variant that deviates in emphasis, constraints, or even overall objective. Rather than overt contradiction, the malicious agent incrementally reshapes the problem space by introducing new priorities or suggesting reinterpretations of the core question, so that group attention gradually shifts from solving $Q$ to addressing $Q'$. 

Let $R(\cdot)$ denote the reasoning trajectory of the agent team. Under successful reframing, the group’s path evolves as $R(Q) \rightarrow R(Q')$, where $R(Q')$ is locally consistent but globally misaligned with the original task goal. The reframing thus induces a high-level cognitive deviation while preserving surface-level linguistic and logical integrity. It may even cause the group to adopt a final answer $O^*_2$ that is unrelated to the original correct solution $O^*_1$.

\paragraph {Fake Injection}
This attack paradigm introduces false information into the group conversation by presenting it as established knowledge, trusted convention, or expert consensus. The malicious agent frames misinformation as background truth using confident, authoritative language that implies widespread agreement or official endorsement. These claims may include pseudo-norms, fabricated technical standards, or non-existent best practices, all phrased without uncertainty. As a result, other agents may unconsciously accept and propagate the false information, allowing it to permeate the group’s reasoning process without detection.

Let $\mathcal{I}$ denote the set of informational units shared among agents during collaboration. The attack aims to insert a false element $\tilde{i} \in \mathcal{I}$ such that it is subsequently treated as a valid premise by multiple agents. Since the injected content does not violate role expectations or task constraints, and appears linguistically natural, it often evades normal detection methods.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
