\includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{figs/illustration/scenario2.png}
\caption{A suboptimal fixation attack diverts the team toward overly conservative solutions that impair efficiency.}
\label{fig:scenario2}
\end{figure}

\paragraph {Execution Delay}  This paradigm aims to hinder task progress by overwhelming the group with excessive detail, redundant analysis, and unnecessary procedural complexity. The malicious agent adopts the persona of a meticulous or risk-averse contributor, using this apparent thoroughness to inject delays into collaborative decision-making. Instead of deviating from the task or providing incorrect content, the agent prolongs discussions by raising peripheral concerns or insisting on exhaustive verification.  

Let $C$ denote the expected collaboration cost under normal conditions, measured in time, token consumption, or computational steps, and $C'$ the cost under adversarial interference. The attack succeeds when $C' \gg C$  and $O \approx O^*$ despite the agent maintaining linguistic coherence and adhering to assigned roles. Over time, this subtle delay degrades overall team efficiency and inflates the resource overhead incurred by both the system and users. This subtle attack paradigm maintains response-level correctness while strategically increasing interaction latency.  Its stealthy nature makes it particularly insidious in settings where inference cost is tied to response duration or token consumption, such as commercial LLM subscription models.

\subsection{Attack Scenarios}

We instantiate our intention-hiding attacks across four representative collaborative scenarios in LLM-based multi-agent systems. These scenarios simulate real-world tasks in which multiple agents engage in cooperative dialogue to solve user-issued problems. For each case, we highlight how the malicious agent embeds covert disruptions without breaking role conformity or linguistic coherence.

\paragraph{Scenario 1 Reframing Misalignment}
This scenario illustrates how a malicious agent subtly shifts the problem focus during group collaboration. As shown before in Figure~\ref{fig:scenario} in Section \ref{sec:intro}, the user initiates the session with a request on task $Q_1$, and the team begins to converge on a valid solution. The adversarial agent (Agent 2), however, steers attention toward an unrequested subtask $Q_2$, presenting it as contextually relevant. Subsequent agents validate and build on $Q_2$, eventually completing the wrong objective. The user, upon receiving an irrelevant answer, expresses dissatisfaction. This case demonstrates the power of reframing: while the outputs remain coherent, the underlying group trajectory diverges from the intended goal.

