\subsection{Evaluation Setup}

To evaluate both the effectiveness and detectability of intention-hiding attacks in LLM-based multi-agent systems, we construct a controlled experimental environment encompassing diverse communication structures, task types, and adversarial paradigms, and apply the \sectionstyleword{AgentXposed} framework under the same conditions.

Each experiment is conducted within a simulated LLM-based multi-agent system, where each agent is instantiated from the same pre-trained GPT-4o-mini model, with the exception of the evaluation phase where GPT-4o is exclusively employed.

\noindent \textbf{Communication Structures.}
We examine three communication structures, each defining a distinct coordination protocol:

\paragraph{Centralized} The system includes a coordinator agent responsible for decomposing the main task and dispatching subtasks to solver agents. After receiving their outputs, a validator agent performs a final check. All information flows through the central node.

\paragraph{Decentralized}  All agents operate as peers with equal authority. They engage in open-ended discussion, independently evaluate each other’s responses, and collectively vote to reach a final decision.

\paragraph{Layered} A hierarchical structure is adopted, where a top-level analyst agent decomposes the task and assigns intermediate subproblems to domain-specific solvers. A validator agent at the bottom layer integrates and checks their responses.

Role prompts are consistent across tasks for the same agent type. Details are provided in Appendix \ref{sec:app}.

% Prompt Example

\noindent \textbf{Task Domains.}
We evaluate six diverse task datasets to reflect different requirements: 
\begin{itemize} 
\item \textbf{MMLU} \cite{hendrycks2020measuring} and \textbf{MMLU-Pro} \cite{wang2024mmlu}: Multiple-choice knowledge and reasoning benchmarks. 
\item \textbf{HumanEval} \cite{chen2021evaluating} and \textbf{GSM8K} \cite{cobbe2021training}: Program synthesis and arithmetic reasoning tasks. 
\item \textbf{Arithmetic} and \textbf{Biographies}: Custom datasets proposed by Du et al. \cite{du2023improving}, designed to evaluate factual numerical reasoning and open-ended descriptive synthesis.
\end{itemize} For each structure and task, we evaluate 100 randomly sampled questions.

\noindent \textbf{Attack and Detection Settings.} Each environment is evaluated under five conditions: (1) a benign baseline with no attack, and (2–5) four intention-hiding paradigms: Suboptimal Fixation, Reframing Misalignment, Fake Consensus Injection, and Execution Delay. In all attack scenarios, a single adversarial agent is embedded while preserving role and prompt consistency. Each attack involves randomly inserting the malicious agent into the team, rather than assigning the adversarial role to a fixed agent or position.

To simplify evaluation, we apply the detection framework to dialogue logs from MMLU tasks under four attack paradigms and a mixed scenario. We additionally include two undisguised baseline attacks for comparison: (i) \textit{Incorrect Fact Injection}, where the malicious agent inserts confidently stated but fabricated factual claims, and (ii) \textit{Dark Trait Injection}, adapted from the PsySafe framework, where the agent's prompt is conditioned on personality profiles characterized by high levels of Machiavellianism, narcissism, and psychopathy.

\noindent \textbf{Evaluation Metrics.}
We report metrics from both task performance and detection perspectives to evaluate the impact and observability of intention-hiding attacks.

\begin{itemize} \item \textbf{Task Accuracy.} The proportion of tasks successfully completed by the multi-agent system, used to measure the degradation caused by each attack paradigm.

\item \textbf{Token Usage.} The average number of tokens consumed per task, broken down by agent roles. This captures communication overhead and inefficiency introduced by covert behaviors.

\item \textbf{Detection Precision / Recall / F1 Score.} For detection experiments, we treat the identification of malicious agents as a binary classification problem. We report standard metrics over repeated runs: precision (true positives over all flagged), recall (true positives over all actual adversaries), and F1 score.

% \item \textbf{False Positive Rate.} The proportion of benign agents incorrectly flagged as adversarial, used to assess the robustness of the detection method under realistic settings with mixed agent roles. 
\end{itemize}

Unless otherwise specified, all metrics are reported with standard deviations. Accuracy on HumanEval is measured using pass@1. We highlight the indicator with the highest fluctuation range in \sethlcolor{best}\hl{light red}, while marking the second one in \sethlcolor{second}\hl{light blue}.

