\subsection{Evaluation of Attack Module}
We begin by examining \textit{whether the insertion of intention-hiding agents disrupts multi-agent collaboration}, as posed in \textit{RQ1}.  To this end, we measure task performance degradation introduced by each attack paradigm across six benchmark datasets, using the benign setting as a baseline.  To investigate \textit{RQ2}, \textit{which concerns the consistency of these effects across different coordination protocols}, we repeat the evaluation under three communication structures:\textit{ Centralized, Decentralized, and Layered.}

\noindent \textbf{Task Performance Degradation for RQ1.}
We assess the performance impact of intention-hiding agents across six task types. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:structure_accuracy}, open-ended generation tasks such as Biographies and HumanEval exhibit the largest accuracy degradation, while multiple-choice tasks like MMLU and MMLU-Pro are comparatively less affected.

Fake Injection introduces the most severe disruption in generative tasks. In the decentralized structure, HumanEval accuracy drops from 84\% to 55\%, and Biographies from 70\% to 45\%. Similar patterns hold in layered setups, where Fake Injection reduces HumanEval from 76\% to 66\% and Biographies from 64\% to 53\%. These tasks rely on shared factual grounding, which may make them susceptible to fabricated information framed as consensus.

Suboptimal Fixation also causes substantial degradation, particularly in  multiple-choice tasks. For instance, MMLU-Pro drops from 57\% to 43\% in centralized settings and from 66\% to 50\% in layered setups.

Reframing Misalignment demonstrates considerable effects in program synthesis and mathematical reasoning tasks, where precise interpretation of problem statements is critical. In the decentralized structure, HumanEval accuracy drops from 84\% to 51\%, and Arithmetic from 62\% to 55\%. By gradually shifting the task framing, the malicious agent leads peers to solve a subtly altered version of the problem. This method may be disruptive in environments where correctness hinges on tightly scoped logical objectives.

Execution Delay has minimal effect on correctness across all tasks, with accuracy consistently close to benign levels. For instance, GSM8K remains at 95\% in layered and decentralized setups. However, this comes at a substantial communication cost. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:token_usage_corrected}, Execution Delay consistently yields the highest token usage across all structures and tasks. This aligns with our design: preserving correctness while strategically hindering progress.

In general, our results indicate that intention-hiding agents are most disruptive in open-ended or semantically fragile tasks, where solution correctness depends on shared understanding or precise framing. Figure~\ref{fig:Column} summarizes the degradation trends across all tasks and attack types.

