\noindent \textbf{Cross-Structural Generalizability for RQ2.}
To evaluate whether intention-hiding attacks generalize across different coordination protocols, we analyze performance degradation under each communication structure: decentralized, centralized, and layered. As reported in Table~\ref{tab:structure_accuracy}, all three structures are impacted by adversarial agents, though the extent and nature of disruption vary with task type and coordination mechanism.

\textit{Decentralized} systems experience the most severe performance decline in open-ended generation tasks. In HumanEval, accuracy drops from 84\% in the benign condition to 55\% under Fake Injection and 51\% under Reframing. In Biographies, the drop reaches 25\%. These systems lack centralized validation, which may make them particularly vulnerable to misinformation and semantic shift propagated through peer discussion. Once distorted content is introduced, it spreads without structural barriers, causing sharp divergence from the intended objective.

\textit{Centralized} systems demonstrate greater robustness in generative tasks, not because they prevent all forms of attack propagation, but because the centralized coordinator dominates task planning and decision-making. In HumanEval, Fake Injection leads to an accuracy drop of 11\%, and Biographies exhibits a similar decline. These systems rely on a central node to dispatch subtasks, which can isolates the influence scope of peripheral agents. Unless the coordinator itself is compromised, the adversarial agent’s capacity to distort task semantics or final outputs may remains limited. However, when the attacker occupies the coordinator role, the system may become highly vulnerable. 

\textit{Layered} systems maintain considerably high robustness across tasks and attacks. In Arithmetic and GSM8K, accuracy remains high under all attack paradigms. In HumanEval and Biographies, the degradation is modest and considerably smaller. The hierarchical separation of roles contains adversarial influence. Top-level agents decompose tasks, and bottom-level validators constrain the spread of semantic deviation, which may result in structurally enforced correction paths.

