\subsection{Evaluation of \sectionstyleword{AgentXposed}}

To address RQ3, we evaluated the ability of \sectionstyleword{AgentXposed} to detect intention-hiding agents across different attack paradigms and communication structures. The threshold value $\tau$ for entering the interrogation phase was set to 1. Table~\ref{tab:metrics_full} presents the detection results in terms of precision, recall, and F1 score. We compare three personality-based detection models: HEXACO, MBTI, and the Big Five, each applied consistently across settings. Figure~\ref{fig:Column2} provides a visual summary of detection performance across all experimental conditions.

\noindent \textbf{Comparison of Personality Models.} We first compare the detection performance of three personality-based models (HEXACO, MBTI, and Big Five) under consistent attack and structure conditions. Across all communication structures and attack types, the HEXACO-based \sectionstyleword{AgentXposed} framework consistently yields the highest F1 scores.

In the layered structure, HEXACO achieves F1 scores of 80.52 for Suboptimal Fixation, 72.97 for Fake Injection, and 70.34 for Execution Delay. These values exceed those of MBTI by 6–9 points and those of Big Five by 10–15 points. The advantage is similar in centralized settings, where HEXACO reaches 77.92 on Suboptimal Fixation and 66.67 on Execution Delay, outperforming MBTI by roughly 5 points and Big Five by over 10 in the latter case. Even in decentralized systems, which exhibit more behavioral variability, HEXACO maintains F1 above 71 across all attacks, while MBTI and Big Five often fall below 67.

Precision and recall metrics also reflect this trend. For example, in the centralized setting under Fake Injection, HEXACO achieves 76.81 precision and 66.25 recall, compared to MBTI’s 71.64 and 60.00. The Big Five model consistently underperforms, with notable deficits in both recall and F1 score, especially on attacks such as Execution Delay and Reframing.

These results suggest that HEXACO provides a more stable representation of agent traits, enabling better discrimination between benign and malicious behavior across diverse tasks. While MBTI and Big Five capture some predictive signal, their performance suffers from less differentiated trait modeling, particularly in high-overlap dialogue contexts.

\noindent \textbf{Detection Performance Analysis for Diverse Attack Types.} Detection performance varies notably across the four intention-hiding attack paradigms. Suboptimal Fixation is consistently the easiest to detect across all settings. HEXACO achieves F1 scores of 80.52 in the layered structure, 77.92 in centralized, and 77.63 in decentralized settings. This may be because the attack embodies a persistent intent to select suboptimal answers. In multiple-choice scenarios where one option is objectively correct, particularly when agents avoid selecting comprehensive answers like \texttt{D: All of the above} while disproportionately focusing on justifying individual sub-answers, their anomalous behavioral patterns diverge markedly from typical responses, making them distinctly identifiable via trait-based assessment metrics.

Fake Injection and Reframing Misalignment yield intermediate detection rates. In layered setups, their F1 scores are 72.97 and 74.17, respectively, with slightly lower values in other structures. These attacks manipulate task framing or content, but do so in ways that retain surface plausibility, resulting in less distinct trait signals.

Execution Delay is the most difficult to detect. F1 drops to 66.67 in centralized and 64.75 in decentralized settings, with recall values falling below 60. This indicates that agents who prolong dialogue without introducing semantic contradictions exhibit minimal observable deviation, reducing detection sensitivity.

In contrast, baseline attacks such as Incorrect Fact Injection and the dark-personality-based prompt design in \textit{Psysafe} yield significantly higher detection rates. In centralized settings, both attacks achieve F1 above 89.0, with recall as high as 95. This suggests that overt semantic violations or malicious tonality are more reliably flagged, while intention-hiding strategies evade detection by mimicking cooperative behavior.

