\subsection{Limitations and Future Work}

\paragraph{Dynamic Agent Roles} Current experiments assume fixed agent roles and static task formats, simplifying detection but limiting real-world applicability. In dynamic multi-agent settings, roles may shift, internal states can be shared, and personas may adapt fluidly \cite{111yang2023adt}. This challenges static personality baselines, necessitating adaptive scoring strategies. We propose exploring ensemble baselining and dynamic thresholding to accommodate evolving group variance.

\paragraph{Multimodal Challenges} Existing evaluations focus on single-modal, language-based collaboration. However, in embodied or multimodal environments, such as visual grounding, real-time coordination, or sensor manipulation, intention-hiding behaviors may manifest non-linguistically \cite{112shi2025muma}. Extending \sectionstyleword{AgentXposed} to these domains will require enhanced behavioral modeling and cross-modal inference.

\section{Ethics Statement}
This work investigates covert attacks and detection mechanisms in LLM-based multi-agent systems, with the primary goal of improving collaborative system safety. All experiments were conducted in fully simulated environments using publicly available or synthetically generated data, ensuring no real-world systems were affected.

To support reproducibility and facilitate further research on LLM-based multi-agent systems safety, we publicly release the code and datasets used in this study. We ensure that the released resources are provided solely for academic use and may not be adapted or deployed for commercial purposes without explicit authorization.


\section{Conclusion}
We present the first systematic investigation of intention-hiding attacks in large language model-based multi-agent systems. We propose four representative paradigms: suboptimal fixation, misalignment of the rearrangement, false injection, and execution delay, and evaluate their disruptive effects across various domains of tasks and coordination structures. Our findings show that such attacks can degrade performance without violating surface-level behavior norms. To address this, we introduce AgentXposed, a psychologically grounded detection framework that outperforms standard trait models in identifying covert adversaries. We further explore how attack impact scales with both team size and discussion depth, revealing that in decentralized environments, malicious influence exhibits cumulative propagation across agents and conversational turns. We hope our work can offer valuable insights for advancing the secure deployment of LLM-based multi-agent systems.

