\textbf{Connection between OT and the monopolist's problem:}
Returning to the monopolist's problem, given a pricing function  $v$ and corresponding competitor $u \in \mathcal{U}$ in  \eqref{PA}, define $\nu =y^*_\#\mu$, representing the distribution of products sold.  Then it is well known that $y^*$ is an optimal map for the optimal transport problem \eqref{eqn: OT primal} with surplus $b$ and marginals $\mu$ and $\nu$,  while $u$ and $v$ solve its dual \eqref{eqn: OT dual}.
Thus, in particular, any solution to the monopolist's problem induces a solution to an optimal transport problem.

\begin{definition}
When $b(x,z)=x\cdot z,$    we say a solution $u$ of the monopolist's problem \eqref{PA} with data $(\mu, Y,c)$ is nested (respectively discretely nested, if  $Y$ is a discrete set), if the optimal transport problem \eqref{eqn: OT primal} with marginals $\mu$ and $Du_{\#}\mu$ is nested (respectively discretely nested).
\end{definition}

