\begin{abstract}
%  \apmarnote{Reworded abstract slightly. Question: do we want to preface the acronym TAP with "the" whenever we mention it? This is what you would say if you were to expand the acronym ("The Target Approximation Problem"), but "the TAP" feels a bit awkward.}%
%  \dlnmarnote{I think we do not. I think we can treat ``TAP'' as expanding as ``(the) Target Approximation Problem'' and sleep okay at night. Also, why did we put ``problem'' into the acronym? Nobody knows. At least we only one time did a ``PIN number'' kind of thing.}
  In many covering settings, it is natural to consider the simultaneous presence of desirable elements (that we seek to include) and undesirable elements (that we seek to avoid).
  This paper introduces a novel combinatorial problem formalizing this tradeoff: from a collection of sets containing both ``desirable'' and ``undesirable'' items, pick the subcollection that maximizes the \emph{margin} between the number of desirable and undesirable elements covered.
  We call this the \emph{Target Approximation Problem} (TAP) and argue that many real-world scenarios are naturally modeled via this objective.
%  \apmarnote{This last bit is begging to be a second paragraph, but I assume that this is an abstract faux pas. Anna/DLN: what do you think?}
%  \dlnmarnote{Agreed. I've seen 2-graf abstracts before and maybe even wrote one, but I think this reads well as a single paragraph. And it's short enough that it's okay.}
  We first show that TAP is hard, even when restricted to cases where the given sets are small or where elements appear in only a small number of sets.
  In a large subset of these cases, we show that TAP is hard to even approximate.
  We then exhibit exact polynomial-time algorithms for other restricted cases and provide an efficient 0.5-approximation for the case where elements occur at most twice, derived through a tight connection to the greedy algorithm for Unweighted Set Cover.
\end{abstract}
