\section{Introduction}

\noindent
Wave optics is an important part of the undergraduate physics curriculum.
Not only does it teach the basics of optics, but it also gives students experience with waves, which are ubiquitous in physics, and it is the prime example of a description of a phenomenon giving a ``deeper'' understanding of another description of the same phenomenon, in this case ray optics.
Understanding wave optics is important for understanding vital concepts such as the resolution of optical telescopes and microscopes.
Wave optics is, at the same time, a very old field of science (the concept of light being a wave is included in Huygens' \emph{Trait\'{e} de la Lumi\`{e}re} \cite{Huygens-1690}, published in 1690) and an active area of research, recently receiving a boost with the advent of metamaterials \cite{Zheludev-Kivshar-2012}, man-made materials with bespoke optical (including wave-optical) properties, and the invention of transformation optics \cite{Ward-Pendry-1996,Pendry-et-al-2006,Leonhardt-2006}, a powerful way of harnessing the new possibilities unlocked by metamaterials.

