An inclined Gaussian beam can be created by passing the beam from a light source of beam type \texttt{Gaussian beam} (which travels parallel to the optical axis) through a wedge with a deflection angle (yz projection) of $0.2^\circ$.
The Gaussian beam had a waist size of 0.5\,mm and a wavelength $\lambda = 632.8$\,nm.
The simulation was performed on a $128 \times 128$ grid representing a physical square of size 1\,cm$\times$1\,cm.
(a)~$(x,y)$ cross sections at propagation distances $z = 0$, 0.5\,m, 1\,m, 1.5\,m, and 2\,m.
The full 1\,cm$\times$1\,cm simulation area is shown.
(b)~$(y,z)$ cross section shown from $z = 0$ to $z=2$\,m.
