Fig.\ \ref{space-periodicity-figure} shows what actually happens.
It shows the simulated intensity cross-section of a Gaussian beam in various transverse planes.
The axis of the Gaussian beam is inclined with respect to the $z$ axis such that, on propagation through positive $z$ distances, the beam's intensity cross-section moves up (it also gets wider).
The figure shows that, as the beam leaves one side of the represented rectangle, it re-appears on the opposite side.
Mathematically, this is because the discrete Fourier transform (DFT), which \emph{Young TIM} uses when calculating the beam propagation, implicitly assumes periodic boundary conditions:
% that the rectangular area represented by the amplitude matrix is the unit cell of a periodic pattern. % it deals with a periodic series of numbers, and that the series of numbers before and after the DFT represent one period.
% This assumption effectively translates into periodic boundary conditions, and so 
what \emph{Young TIM} is actually simulating is one unit cell in an infinite lattice, periodic in the $x$ and $y$ directions, and when light leaves the unit cell to one side, light from the neighbouring unit cell on the opposite side also leaves that neighbouring unit cell and enters the cell simulated by \emph{Young TIM}.

