\section*{Conclusions}

\noindent
We initially started to write \emph{Young TIM} as a research tool, but quickly realised its usefulness in teaching of wave optics.
Now \emph{Young TIM} is firmly embedded in a number of experiments in our undergraduate laboratory.
In addition to the benefits for performing additional experiments when time, budget, or even the laws of physics would otherwise not allow this, we believe this experience of complex simulation software run alongside the experiment gives students a more realistic experience of physics research.
Additionally, it might find use in encouraging students to prepare before starting an experiment, possibly in combination with a pre-experiment formative assessment (such as Just-in-Time Teaching (JiTT) \cite{Novak-et-al-1999}).
Finally, our students work in pairs, and one should not underestimate the value of running a simulation alongside the experiment for keeping all students busy.

