% In the universe, open clusters are, in essence, groups of stars that are formed together from the same molecular cloud and stay tied together through a loose gravitational attraction. Ordinarily, they are in clusters of tens to thousands of stars in galactic disks, and the stars among them typically share a similar age range, chemical compositions, and distances. Throughout the recent history of observational astronomy using large spectroscopic surveys, open clusters have proven to be crucial barometers to study stellar and galactic chemical evolution as well as to trace key astronomical parameters, such as the galactic chemical gradients, tracking iron abundance (Fe/H), alpha element abundances ([$\alpha$/Fe]), and other elemental ratios - Ba, Eu, Na, Mg, Si, Ca, Ti. 

Open clusters are a fundamental building block of galactic disks, making them an excellent tool for Galactic archaeology. 
In the last two decades, large spectroscopic surveys such as the Apache Point Observatory Galactic Evolution Experiment \citep[APOGEE,][]{apogee}, GALactic Archaeology with Hermes \citep[GALAH,][]{galah}, \gaia-ESO \citep[][]{gaia_eso}, and Large sky Area Multi-Object fiber Spectroscopic Telescope \citep[LAMOST,][]{lamost}, 
