have made it possible to build upon the work of \citet{janes_79} to further explore and constrain the Galactic chemical gradient for a variety of element species, including $\alpha$, iron-peak, odd-z and neutron capture.  
Numerous studies \citep[e.g.,][]{yang2025, occasoV, magrini_23, gaia_grads, myers_2022, spina_21, occam_p4, magrini_2017, occam_katia, frinchaboy_13} have utilized these surveys and proven open clusters to be crucial barometers to study stellar evolution, calibrate key astronomical parameters, and trace Galactic chemical evolution. The majority of these studies have fit the radial metallicity gradient using a two-component fit with a free parameter transition point \citep{yang2025,occasoV,magrini_23,myers_2022,occam_p4}, but whether a bilinear fit out performs a single linear fit is still up for debate. 

