Open clusters are ideal tracers of chemical abundance patterns for a variety of reasons.
They are coeval \citep{1995ARA&A..33..381F}; the stellar population within open clusters form nearly simultaneously from the same molecular cloud, allowing for a more precise determination of the ages of stars. They are also largely chemically homogeneous, with a typical scatter of $\sim$0.02 dex or less in most elements as seen in observational studies \citep[e.g.,][]{sinha_24,2016ApJ...817...49B}, as well as in simulated FIRE galaxies \citep[e.g.,][]{bhattarai_24}.  

