The last open cluster membership entry in the Open Cluster Chemical Abundance and Mapping (OCCAM) survey, \citet{myers_2022}, based on SDSS-IV/APOGEE DR17 \citep{dr17}, included 153 clusters, 94 of which were deemed high quality. As the number of clusters available for analysis increases, we can begin to investigate the nature of these azimuthal variations across the Galactic disk. 
Recent studies, \citep[e.g.,][]{hackshaw_2024,hawkins_23,poggio_22} have investigated these azimuthal variations using field giant stars. \citet{hawkins_23} and \citet{poggio_22} found evidence that the variations are correlated with the spiral arm structure. However, \citet{hackshaw_2024} found that the connection between azimuthal variations in the metallicity gradient and spiral arm structure to be inconclusive. 
Follow up studies leveraging the ever-growing open cluster sample will augment the field star work and help to clarify the ambiguities still present. 

